-----BEGIN SQLTRIAGE UPDATE SIGNING KEY PLACEHOLDER-----
This is NOT a real public key. It is a placeholder shipped so the build
compiles before the code-signing certificate is dropped in.

While this placeholder is in place, UpdateSignatureVerifier.IsTrustedKeyConfigured
returns false and ALL update-signature checks FAIL CLOSED (no update can be
staged or applied). Combined with "Updates:Enabled": false in client/test
appsettings, the updater is fully inert.

!! DO NOT write the literal PEM header string in this file. !!
UpdateSignatureVerifier.LoadEmbeddedTrustedKey decides "is a real key embedded?"
by a plain substring search for that header. An earlier version of this file
spelled the header out in the prose below, which made IsTrustedKeyConfigured
report TRUE while this placeholder was still in place - the exact opposite of
the documented contract. (VerifyFile still failed closed, so no update was ever
wrongly trusted, but the "signing is configured" signal was a lie.)
Removed 2026-07-21.

TO GO LIVE (drop-in once the cert is in the certificate store):
  1. tools\extract-update-pubkey.ps1 -Thumbprint <sha1-of-your-codesigning-cert>
     -> overwrites this file with the real SubjectPublicKeyInfo PEM block
  2. Rebuild (the real key is embedded into the assembly at COMPILE time).
  3. Release with: publish-release.ps1 -SigningThumbprint <sha1>
  4. Set "Updates:Enabled": true in the shipped config/appsettings.json.

NOTE ON THE CERTIFICATE: the production cert is a Certum "Open Source Code
Signing in the Cloud" certificate. Its private key is in Certum's HSM and can
never be exported, so there is no .pfx. Step 1 needs only the PUBLIC key, so it
works without a SimplySign session; steps 3 requires SimplySign logged in.

The verifier auto-detects RSA or ECDSA from the PEM, so any standard
code-signing certificate works without code changes.
-----END SQLTRIAGE UPDATE SIGNING KEY PLACEHOLDER-----
